my name is Adam Bello I'm a book editor  at HarperCollins and I'm the editor of a  new book published by the Templeton  Press called new threats to freedom  it wasn't until I sat down and read the  whole stack of essays from beginning to  end that I began to see some patterns  one of the one of the recurring themes  for example is the concern about the  abuse of the idea of fairness this was  something that has been bothering me you  know ever since the Obama election  because we're constantly being told that  you know that we need more fairness and  so I you know I did explicitly ask one  or two people that to tackle this idea  and other people just spontaneously went  there on their own so it does seem to me  that this is one of those you know one  of those buzzwords that has entered into  the public lexicon that that whose  content really needs to be unpacked and  critically examined  so that was one thread that runs through  the through the book but I think taken  as a whole  the thing that I like about the  collection is its idiosyncrasy the fact  that it's a collection of essays by  people who are very distinct in their  way of thinking and writing and  expressing themselves they typically  don't belong to any school of thought I  was I particularly went out of my way to  commissioned pieces from literary  authors people like Mark Halperin the  novelist David Mamet the playwright  Shelby Steele who was somebody I  consider to be a literary intellectual  though he doesn't produce literature  because I because I've always felt that  that the literary mind has a sort of  breadth of humane concern that we don't  often find in the precincts of an  ideological shop  the more successful we've been as a  civilization the more the more we tend  to as Machiavelli would say we mistake  our our good luck for our virtue and  strength there's a there's a tendency to  to think well we came out on top because  it was somehow destined our history is  on our side or you know we're number one  where we're the best and you know I  think we have to take seriously always  the the admonition of our founders that  Liberty is our own individual burden  privilege and responsibility the  generation that that I refer to as the  grown-ups the generation of my father  Solzhenitsyn the the the great figures  of the Cold War the intellect of  intellectual heroes of the Cold War the  people that I admired and looked up to  when I was a when I was a young book  editor that those people are gone now  and I did and I did feel as i canta came  to feel as i was putting this collection  together that I was looking to sort of  you know replace them in some way we I  felt that we needed to step up as a  generation to this challenge of reviving  a discourse about freedom and democracy  but I wanted it to be also different in  the sense that you know my perception of  you know what it was like to sit around  the dinner table with people like you  know my father and and his friends was  somewhat gloomy you know perhaps because  the threat of nuclear annihilation was  you know was always hanging over us and  there was that there was that sense of  urgency of ultimate stakes I don't feel  that the United States is in danger of  being you know annihilated  for example but i but i do feel that we  have an opportunity and an obligation as  a generation to revive this concern this  traditional concern with freedom but  that we we can also do it in a way  that's more consistent with our temper  as a generation we are we're more I  think you know comfortable with popular  culture there's a lot more levity in our  in our generation and so I wanted the  our way of embracing these issues to be  a little less doer and grim and a little  more upbeat more fun the defense of  freedom should be fun  you
 

